<!-- Phase 5 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase5-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">You rename a user on the detail screen. The mutation succeeds, but the list still shows the old name. Fetching fewer fields did not solve consistency. GraphQL’s next idea is identity: if two operations describe the same entity, can the client update one shared record and notify the readers that depend on it?</p>

<!-- @before Internal Mechanics :: review -->
The comment syntax in the GetUserProfile.graphql block (GQ1) is under technical review. A correction is pending the owner's approval.

<!-- @diagram f99fb81083ab1479 -->
### Build inputs and runtime data are different paths

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Schema input</strong><small>reviewed build artifact</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Operation document</strong><small>selected fields</small></div></div><div class="down"></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Apollo compiler</strong><small>validates operations → generates operation models</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>ApolloClient ↔ server</strong><small>runtime operation and response</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Generated models</strong><small>used to parse that response</small></div></div><div class="node"><strong>Optional normalized cache</strong><small>client writes response records</small></div></div><figcaption>The runtime server is separate from the pinned schema used during the build. The original diagram remains available below.</figcaption></figure>

