<!-- @after Production Example :: animation -->
### The same user through two selections

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">A cache write reaches an affected watcher</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="list"><strong>List operation watcher</strong><small data-text="listV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="detail"><strong>Rename mutation</strong><small data-text="detailV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="record"><strong>Normalized record User:7</strong><small data-text="recordV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Read",
"caption": "Assume the cache-key policy identifies this user as User:7, and the list selects its name.",
"state": {
"list": "active",
"record": "done"
},
"text": {
"listV": "observing name",
"detailV": "idle",
"recordV": "name = Ana"
},
"add": [
"list",
"detail",
"record"
]
},
{
"label": "Rename",
"caption": "The mutation returns the same entity identity and the updated selected field.",
"state": {
"detail": "active"
},
"text": {
"detailV": "User:7, name = Anika"
}
},
{
"label": "Write",
"caption": "Writing that response changes the shared record rather than an unrelated copy.",
"state": {
"record": "active"
},
"text": {
"recordV": "name = Anika"
}
},
{
"label": "Notify",
"caption": "An affected watcher can re-emit after the cache write. The UI decides how to display the result.",
"state": {
"list": "active",
"detail": "done"
},
"text": {
"listV": "new result with Anika"
}
},
{
"label": "Missing identity",
"caption": "If identity or the relevant field is absent, do not expect this update path automatically; plan an explicit update or refetch.",
"state": {
"record": "warn"
},
"text": {
"recordV": "identity and selected fields matter"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>This is a cache-dependency example, not a guarantee that every mutation updates every screen. HTTP caching is a separate mechanism.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Misconceptions :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>HTTP 200 contains a user and a field error. Must the screen discard the user?</summary>

That depends on the operation’s product contract. GraphQL can return partial data with errors; explicitly map usable content and stable failures rather than deciding from HTTP status alone.
</details>

<details><summary>The build validated yesterday’s schema. Can today’s server still break a shipped app?</summary>

Yes. Build validation checks the artifact supplied to that build. Server compatibility and supported-client operation checks must protect already-shipped binaries.
</details>

<details><summary>Two objects both have a field named id. Is shared cache identity automatic?</summary>

Do not assume it. Check the configured cache-key policy and the returned entity/field selection. A cache integration test should demonstrate the cross-operation update the product depends on.
</details>
